{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794588724,"nonce":"K/704MSVwY27rjJeSnYLRioGUA4=","title":"A wake the loop asked for in 16 ms arrives 0.2 to 1 s late, now and then","message":"Seen in the same windowed runs of flip_window_check. After a drawn frame that asked for another in 16 ms (wake_in_ms 16 in BLINC_MOTION_TRACE, WakeProxy::wake_at), no frame ran for 657 ms after the second frame and for 1,002 ms later in one run, 604 ms and 177 ms in another, 208 ms in a third. Most runs have none over 200 ms, and a run of three had only 112 and 173 ms ones. The gaps carry no log lines at all, so the loop was not running; the frame that ends one has a normal cost.\n\nWhat is known: wake_at is a condvar timer thread that sends AppCommand::Wake, which the app turns into a redraw. It looks right and is not shown to be the late part. blinc_platform_desktop::wakes=trace shows the wake cause but no wake lines inside a gap. Not tried: a timestamp at the timer thread when it sends, against one at user_event when winit delivers it, which would say whether the timer, the event delivery or the redraw is late; running with the window frontmost and with it covered, to see whether the OS throttles an occluded or background window; the examples drive the loop by request_animation_tick, which spins it (59d424e), so a run without that is the cleaner control.\n\nNot looked at beyond that, since nothing here reproduces it on demand.\n\nRelated: 77fc97d, 59d424e.","files":null}]}